  "mechanisms": [
    "When yaw is unyoked to the path, detected rotation is mostly attributed to a rotation of the trajectory, leading to misperception.",
    "Illusory percepts arise when optic flow resembles another movement, and insufficient information exists to choose between alternatives, leading to the perception of the simplest matching manoeuvre.",
    "Landmarks provide additional visual and/or extra-retinal information (from eye-movements) that helps disambiguate ego-motion components (translation or rotation) depending on the manoeuvre type.",
    "The structuring of an environment (additional elements beyond basic dots) needs to fulfill certain conditions to be useful, depending on the simulated manoeuvre."
  ],
  "moderators": [
    "Simulated translation length (short vs. long)",
    "Landmark type (shifting vs. fixated)",
    "Proportion of simulated yaw relative to path rotation",
    "Complexity of manoeuvre"
  ],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "Adding a single landmark can improve perception of ego-motion, particularly for linear+yaw conditions and specific curvilinear manoeuvres, suggesting its utility in virtual environments for navigation training or simulation."
  ],
